    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new helper sits in the right unit (this normalizer is the declared single source of truth for E.164 rules), is well-named, documented, and called additively \u2014 that's all good. One structural coherence issue:\n\n- **Duplicated country-code literal.** `hasValidNationalLength` hardcodes `\"61\"` in `e164Digits.startsWith(\"61\")`, but the file already names that value right above as `DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE = \"61\"` (util/TelephoneNormalizer.java:36). The new code should reuse the constant (`e164Digits.startsWith(DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE)`) so Australia's code lives in exactly one place; otherwise a future change to the default has to be remembered in two spots.\n\n- **Coupled magic numbers.** The `- 2` / `- 1` offsets silently encode the country-code lengths (\"61\" \u2192 2, \"1\" \u2192 1), and `== 9` / `== 10` the required national lengths. Deriving the offset from the prefix length (e.g. `DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE.length()`) or at minimum a brief comment tying `2` to `\"61\"` would keep the two literals from drifting apart and make the intent self-evident to a maintainer.\n\nNeither is a logic objection \u2014 purely about keeping the two recognized country codes expressed coherently rather than as scattered literals.",
